WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.922
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.027 --> 00:00:15.300
EDGERTON: Now,
gentlemen and ladies,

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.060
the electronic flash
equipment always

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:24.990
consists of three parts, an
energy source and a device

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.100
for storing the energy
and discharging suddenly

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.800
into a converter, which
we call a flash lamp.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.750
The object of this
talk this morning

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.230
is to discuss flash
lamps to help prepare you

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:41.240
for the experiment that you're
going to do on flash lamps.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.610
Electrically, a flash lamp is
an extremely interesting thing.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.730
Because it's completely
non-linear in every possible

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:48.870
way, shape, and form.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.390
And every lamp is individual.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.470
It's different than other lamps.

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.810
So when you get
through experimenting,

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.640
I hope that you
will be convinced

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.630
that this type of device
does have these variations.

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:03.690
Here's a typical
electronic flash lamp.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.020
This is one of the large
real powerful ones that's

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:09.210
used for stimulating lasers.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.540
It's just, discharges capacitor,
a very large capacitor

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.496
with an inductor and series.

00:01:14.496 --> 00:01:16.770
And one of the biggest
uses of flash tubes

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:21.400
is for making laser lights.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.780
If I put a high voltage on
this, I can break it down.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.740
You can see the
discharge in the device.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.680
There's about 40,000
volts coming out of this.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.210
That's the kind of electricity
that doesn't hurt you.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:37.810
You don't treat other kinds of--

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:40.360
electricity the same way.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:41.780
I did that for effect.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:45.010
You can try this yourself.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:48.570
Don't jump too high when
you try it the first time.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.529
I have a small flash tube
on this display equipment.

00:01:51.529 --> 00:01:53.320
This is one of them
that you will be using.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.569
There are four equipments
like this in the laboratory.

00:01:55.569 --> 00:01:59.860
So no one can, three of you
can work simultaneously.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:01.300
And I have a flash tube on here.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:05.730
And I'm, you'll notice there's
a sound associated with this.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.926
When the sound is on,
the juice is going in.

00:02:07.926 --> 00:02:08.800
And there's a variac.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.930
And I'm watching the voltage,
2,000 volts, 2,500 volts,

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:18.010
as high as I can go on
this particular unit.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:19.480
And of course, nothing happens.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:24.670
Because I am below the
self-starting voltage.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:27.820
Every flash lamp has a
self-starting voltage.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.937
And usually we cannot reach this
in the laboratory with without

00:02:30.937 --> 00:02:32.020
going to a lot of trouble.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.180
The self-starting voltage
on that little lamp

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:36.400
is probably over 4,000 volts.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.300
So it'll be off of this scale.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.180
But I can make the
lamp go at my command

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:46.230
by putting on a stimulator.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.510
By an external electrode,
it breaks down.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:55.460
And if you look
closely, you can see

00:02:55.460 --> 00:03:01.070
a discharge that follows the
same path as the spark wire.

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:05.120
This is a very important
aspect of this whole concept,

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.750
is be able to control the
flash to occur at the time

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.350
as you wish it to occur.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:15.690
Now I'll put on my
little spark coil.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.720
And I will turn
on a high voltage

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.010
and bring it up to 2,500 volts.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.320
And I'll turn on the spark.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.240
And of course, nothing
happens because I'm

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.310
down here at too low voltage.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.130
So I'll run the voltage
up with this tenuator.

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:39.115
This controls the frequency
at which it flashes.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:48.430
So that's 10.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:49.670
That's about 2KV.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.280
That starts very good.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.480
2KV down here at
about 3,500 volts,

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.080
so I get a point right there.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:58.410
Now I want to go down
to about 2,000 volts

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:00.720
where this tube is normally
supposed to be used--

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:09.640
2,000 volts, maybe
a little bit less.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:13.240
Now I have to put more
voltage on to make it go,

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.440
going up, going up.

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:22.400
Thanks, ouch, it's still on.

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:28.364
There we go.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:35.190
I've got about 2,000
volts on there now.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.430
And the spark voltage
is still very reliable.

00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:50.070
Now this is the
point, about this,

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:56.350
I want you to understand
is that reliability is bad.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:59.080
So sometimes it goes.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:00.140
Sometimes it doesn't go.

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:01.780
So I'm up around 2,000 volts.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:08.740
And I'm putting on, according to
this curve, about 3,000 volts,

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:10.570
somewhere in here.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:12.010
And I get some uncertainties.

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:18.430
Let's make, if down here we
get zeros, up here we get Xs.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:21.400
But we get some
zeros in between.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.430
If I go to 1KV, let's
see what happens then.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.690
You're going to have a lot
of fun turning these knobs,

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:42.019
trying to figure
out what's going on.

00:05:42.019 --> 00:05:42.560
There we are.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:50.150
That's, the uncertainty
point is around 30

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:55.310
on here, which is about 6,000
volts, so up in here somewhere,

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:58.310
the uncertainty value for 1KV.

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:02.090
There is a curve,
a bottom curve,

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:10.650
which delineates the
no go area, no go.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:12.260
This is a place
to stay away from.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:13.920
There's another
area which is maybe.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:23.300
And up here is yes, go.

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:28.340
Well, if you ever design
a flash equipment,

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:30.410
you want it to be
in this area and be

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:32.040
up here in good and strong.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:33.730
So it never gets
down into this point.

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:35.450
There's nothing
more discouraging

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.490
than to work marginally
with a flash unit

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:39.590
where you haven't
got enough spark.

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:44.510
So the rule here is put plenty
plenty of zip into this thing

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:46.520
so that it will go.

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:49.070
Now the object of
this first experiment,

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:51.920
I haven't read the sheet,
is to determine that curve

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:53.530
for one of these tubes.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:57.650
Most these tubes, I think
we've got enough for everybody.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:01.100
You'll be able to select out of
this pile one of these tubes.

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:02.870
And there are many
different shapes

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:05.120
and sizes and dimensions.

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:11.300
And you can have the tube
that you test if you want it.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:14.120
You have your opportunity of
testing as many of these tubes

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:14.670
as you want.

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:16.447
If there's a dud,
leave it in the box.

00:07:16.447 --> 00:07:17.030
Don't take it.

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:18.750
But only take a good one.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:20.390
How do you tell a good tube?

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:22.550
First of all, it should
be able to start at,

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:26.390
have a minimum starting
voltage somewhere down in here

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:27.740
around 400 volts.

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:32.240
If it will not start at
400 volts, throw it out.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:33.830
In other words, and
also if you have

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:36.800
one that starts with a
3,000 or 4,000 volts on it,

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:38.010
throw it out.

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:39.780
What you want is
to get a tube that

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:42.140
will be in the range
of which you want

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:44.370
to use that particular tube.

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:50.200
The electrical
characteristics of the tubes

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:51.850
are fascinating to me.

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:54.260
Of course, the light output
is the most important thing.

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:57.220
But as electrical people,
we must learn something

00:07:57.220 --> 00:08:00.640
about how they
operate electrically.

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:02.530
You'll notice in
this display, there's

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:05.620
a very nice little
filament of electricity

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:08.890
that tends to follow
the starting wire.

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:13.680
If I put more energy in,
this film, it gets bigger.

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:27.920
And also gives quite a bit more
light, and it fills the tube.

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:29.630
And that, of course,
is what we want.

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:32.480
When we use a lamp, we
want it to be efficient.

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:38.299
And we want to get lots and
lots of light out of it.

00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:41.270
Now, the electrical
characteristics of these tubes

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:44.250
are shown in a slide that
will come on the screen.

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:53.160
This is a slide of time
versus the voltage across.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:55.760
The tube is the upper curve.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:58.050
You'll notice it's marked wrong.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:02.970
The current rises to a peak
and then decays to zero.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:06.030
Usually the voltage comes down
and leaves a residual voltage

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:10.710
on the capacitor.

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.200
Now every flash
tube it should take

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:16.270
will have a different
characteristic of its type.

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:19.102
Some of these have these
little ripples in them.

00:09:19.102 --> 00:09:20.310
And they're always different.

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:23.960
And this is one thing that
makes this type of experimenting

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:27.810
interesting is because
you never know for sure

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:29.520
what is going to come out.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:32.100
As electrical engineers,
we're in such a habit

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:38.280
of depending on Ohm's law and
the other electrical waves

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:40.530
which calculates circuits
that we try to do everything

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:42.492
we can by calculation.

00:09:42.492 --> 00:09:43.950
But when you work
with flash lamps,

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:46.860
you have to back off
every once in a while

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:50.500
and go to an analog
solution of the equations.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:53.310
It's determined by what
the tube itself does.

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:58.770
The laws of the tube have not
been completely studied yet.

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:05.022
So we have to do as best we can
by calculating from examples

00:10:05.022 --> 00:10:06.480
and then rushing
to the laboratory.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:09.315
And in a few microseconds,
out comes the answer.

00:10:09.315 --> 00:10:11.190
And it's usually different
than what you had.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:15.170
But that is the real answer
for that particular tube.

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:17.580
So this is why people that
work with electronic flash

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:18.997
do lots and lots
of experimenting.

00:10:18.997 --> 00:10:20.704
Because you have to
be in the laboratory.

00:10:20.704 --> 00:10:23.310
And you have to do a lot of
these things on these nonlinear

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:26.070
devices by using the
thing itself in order

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:29.990
to find out what is going on.

00:10:29.990 --> 00:10:35.160
The second slide
shows the same data

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:38.400
plotted on a volt ampere curve.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:39.450
On the bottom is current.

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:41.370
On the top is volts.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:44.100
At the upper left
hand corner, it

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:46.470
starts with the maximum voltage
you have a cross a tube,

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:48.720
such as 2,000 volts.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.060
Then when the current
starts, this current

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:54.610
rises very rapidly to
thousands of amperes.

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:58.260
And then it decays slowly
as the capacitor discharges.

00:10:58.260 --> 00:10:59.955
And it comes down to
a residual voltage,

00:10:59.955 --> 00:11:03.960
a small voltage is
left on the capacitor.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.680
Now, if the tube did
follow Ohm's law,

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:09.369
then you could draw
a resistance line.

00:11:09.369 --> 00:11:10.910
Let's go back to
the slide once more.

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:13.020
There is a straight
line in that.

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:16.190
Can you back up to that resist--

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:17.420
back up to the other slide.

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:19.460
There is a resistance there.

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:25.640
You'll notice that straight
line is a resistance line.

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:28.340
And everybody that works with
flash units gets into a habit

00:11:28.340 --> 00:11:31.809
very quickly of calculating
an approximate resistance.

00:11:31.809 --> 00:11:33.350
You know that the
answers that you're

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.580
going to get with resistance
are going to be wrong.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:37.070
But they're better than nothing.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:38.520
And so you use them.

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:41.390
And one way in which to
calculate this resistance is

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:44.420
to use the maximum voltage
you have on the capacitor,

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:46.220
divide it by the
maximum current that you

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:48.074
get during a discharge.

00:11:48.074 --> 00:11:49.490
And you'll notice
that that curve,

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:53.300
that straight line intersects
the real volt ampere

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:54.920
curve in several places.

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:57.170
And it gives you a
sort of an average.

00:11:57.170 --> 00:11:59.457
And this is a useful concept.

00:11:59.457 --> 00:12:01.040
Because if you don't
know the voltage,

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:02.300
you do know the
resistance, the tube

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:03.966
is defined that way,
you can immediately

00:12:03.966 --> 00:12:09.740
calculate the maximum current,
as long as the current is

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:12.260
limited entirely by the tube.

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:14.840
Now the people that
stimulate lasers

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:16.970
also put an inductor in there.

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:19.280
So this whole thing collapses.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:22.010
Because the inductor is what
determines the maximum peak

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:24.320
current that flows.

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:26.870
But the resistance
concept is still useful

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:30.890
because it enables you to
estimate for a capacitor

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:36.400
discharge the time in
which the thing collapses.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:38.150
The next characteristic
I want to show you

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:39.620
is the one that you've
already experimented

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:40.578
with in the laboratory.

00:12:40.578 --> 00:12:42.850
And you know this
from old times,

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:47.090
is the voltage from a
photo cell that tells you

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:49.470
the number of
[INAUDIBLE],, usually

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:53.240
in the millions of [INAUDIBLE]
as a function of time.

00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:56.680
These curves have several
bumps and ripples on them.

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:58.990
They rise to a peak
value in a certain time

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:02.470
and it takes to
discharge to rise.

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:03.990
And then it decays
exponentially,

00:13:03.990 --> 00:13:07.060
in an exponential form to zero.

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:10.480
Well those of us that
work in this field define

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:14.920
flash duration as the time
between third peak, which

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:18.730
is shown very clearly in this
slide that shows the time

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:19.570
duration of a flash.

00:13:22.540 --> 00:13:24.450
Now how can all
this information be

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:28.110
presented into a form
where you can use it

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:31.140
for designing other tubes
or for understanding

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:32.970
some kind of a tube?

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:38.170
This has all been, all
information on a standard tube

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:43.000
has been assembled on this
sheet that I'll hand you out

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:44.320
at the end of the class.

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:46.455
It shows the dimensions
of the flash tube.

00:13:46.455 --> 00:13:48.580
And we're going to look at
these curves on the view

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:52.240
graph in a minute and try
to explain to you what

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:58.270
they mean so that you'll be
able to use this information.

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:00.790
The lamp on which this
experimental work was done

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:06.400
was a six inch lamp, about that
long, of this four millimeter

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:09.160
inside diameter,
filled with xenon gas

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:11.140
at about 20 centimeters.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:14.320
That's about a fifth
of an atmosphere.

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:16.300
It's excited externally.

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:18.670
And the variables that
you have are the voltage

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:21.430
across the tube,
that capacitance you

00:14:21.430 --> 00:14:24.050
have connected across the tube.

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:25.870
And you want to
know a lot of things

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:27.430
about this when you
want to apply it

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:29.230
to some particular problem.

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:34.140
So I'll go over the view graph
and show you these curves.

00:14:37.615 --> 00:14:39.490
Well, these are things
that we've picked out.

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:40.720
Maybe they're the right things.

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:41.470
Maybe they're not.

00:14:41.470 --> 00:14:43.319
But the first one that
we're interested in

00:14:43.319 --> 00:14:44.860
is, how much peak
current do you get?

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:47.530
If you're designing a circuit,
how many thousands of amperes

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:48.370
do you get?

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:55.210
Well, here is a curve
of the peak current.

00:14:55.210 --> 00:15:00.080
This goes up to
about 3,000 amperes.

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:01.600
And it's done for
capacities that

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:05.980
go from one microfarad to 200
microfarad and voltages that go

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:11.380
from 500 volts to 3,500 volts.

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:14.260
So there's a tremendous
range of things that

00:15:14.260 --> 00:15:17.320
are covered in this experiment.

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:20.260
And I think you realize
that the data for this

00:15:20.260 --> 00:15:22.300
was obtained by going
into the laboratory

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:25.210
and measuring the peak light
as a function of these things

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:27.280
and mounting them
on this log plot

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:31.590
so that you could get the whole
data on that particular chart.

00:15:31.590 --> 00:15:34.360
The full rated value
of that particular tube

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:36.910
is 100 microfarad
at 2,000 volts.

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:41.317
And so here, 2,000 volts,
you read 1,200 amperes.

00:15:41.317 --> 00:15:43.150
And if you want to know
the lamp resistance,

00:15:43.150 --> 00:15:46.000
you divide 2,000 by 1,200.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:54.740
And it's equal to approximately
2 ohms, 2 ohms, close enough.

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:58.100
So you say that is a 2 ohm lamp.

00:15:58.100 --> 00:16:03.410
And from then on, you can
deal with it as a 2 ohm lamp.

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:05.965
Other things that are of
interest is the peak light.

00:16:05.965 --> 00:16:08.132
If you're doing experiment
in the laboratory

00:16:08.132 --> 00:16:10.340
and you want to know how
many millions of candlepower

00:16:10.340 --> 00:16:12.290
are coming out,
you can measure it.

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:17.750
And you go from one million,
10 million, 100 million,

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:20.390
doesn't go up that high,
with same way capacitors.

00:16:20.390 --> 00:16:23.990
Go from one microfarad
to 200 microfarad.

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:25.820
And the voltage
covers the same range.

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:31.670
This again covers a tremendous
range of light over this range.

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:34.850
Now, this standard tube
at full load rating

00:16:34.850 --> 00:16:40.020
again reads eight
million, 500, 8.5 times 10

00:16:40.020 --> 00:16:42.500
to the sixth candlepower light.

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:44.990
Quite often, when we wish to
test the photoelectric cell

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:50.150
in a laboratory or some device,
we drag out this six inch tube.

00:16:50.150 --> 00:16:53.425
We put a capacitor of
100 microfarad across it

00:16:53.425 --> 00:16:54.050
at 2,000 volts.

00:16:54.050 --> 00:16:55.370
We flash it.

00:16:55.370 --> 00:16:58.680
And out will come
eight and a half

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:01.370
million candlepower,
which makes it easy

00:17:01.370 --> 00:17:05.450
for us to do our experiments.

00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:08.889
This curve is very important,
one flash duration.

00:17:08.889 --> 00:17:10.430
Because the first
thing that you wish

00:17:10.430 --> 00:17:16.460
to know when you do any
experiments on a subject,

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:20.390
to stop its motion, you need to
know what the flash duration.

00:17:20.390 --> 00:17:23.560
The flash duration is too
long, you'll get a blur.

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:27.089
So here is flash duration
running from 10 microseconds

00:17:27.089 --> 00:17:35.154
up to 500 microseconds for
capacities going from 1 to 100.

00:17:35.154 --> 00:17:36.570
For the different
voltages, you'll

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:39.580
notice that there's a
tendency for it to be flat.

00:17:39.580 --> 00:17:43.230
It would be flat if this
resistance were constant.

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:44.370
But it's not constant.

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:46.620
The resistance goes up as
you go to lower voltages.

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:48.270
And that means
these curves go up.

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:51.030
And the discharge lasts longer.

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:53.640
In other words, if you have a
small capacitor on a flash lamp

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:59.850
like I showed you, you will
have a nice little, skinny, dim

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:02.430
filament, which
doesn't go anywhere

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:08.100
near as high and light level
and it lasts a much longer time.

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:10.445
So this curve is
a very useful one.

00:18:10.445 --> 00:18:11.820
Now, the final
curve, and I think

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:16.200
one of the most useful ones,
is the one of efficiency.

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:22.890
Efficiency is given on the
axis in candlepower per watt.

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:25.050
If you could double the
efficiency of a flash tube,

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:27.750
it would be a great thing.

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:31.140
So how do you get the
efficiency higher than 5?

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:32.640
I just don't know.

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:34.890
But anyway, these
are the highs as far

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:36.340
as we've been able to get it.

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:37.839
But you'll notice
that they come up.

00:18:37.839 --> 00:18:41.634
Again, these are a function
of capacity and voltage.

00:18:41.634 --> 00:18:43.050
And you would think
that you would

00:18:43.050 --> 00:18:49.410
be able to design all the flash
units to be efficiency of 5.

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:51.990
Well, that's a good idea.

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:55.290
We should keep working on
it and try to get those up.

00:18:55.290 --> 00:18:58.260
So I think these four curves,
you'll have copies of these

00:18:58.260 --> 00:18:59.550
to take home with you.

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:04.020
And you can check these
in the lab or as you wish.

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:07.320
And you can help to use these
for designing other flash

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:08.760
tubes.

00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:11.220
All the information on
designing other flash tubes

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:16.160
is given in this book that
you've probably heard about.

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:19.900
And the rules are quite
simple on how to do this.

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:25.350
Suppose you want to make
your tube twice as long.

00:19:25.350 --> 00:19:28.830
The physics of the
tube require that you

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:33.490
have exactly the same
voltage per unit length.

00:19:33.490 --> 00:19:37.920
So if you have a 6 inch
tube with 2,000 volts on it

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:42.660
and you make a 12 inch tube, how
many volts do you need on it?

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:44.400
STUDENT: 4,000.

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:47.320
INSTRUCTOR: Hooray, 4,000.

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:50.620
If you put 100 watt
seconds into a 6 inch tube,

00:19:50.620 --> 00:19:52.990
how many watt seconds do you
have to put in the others?

00:19:52.990 --> 00:19:54.670
The other requirement,
you have to put

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:57.410
that same number of watt
seconds per cubic centimeter

00:19:57.410 --> 00:19:59.110
in these two tubes.

00:19:59.110 --> 00:20:02.560
So you'd have to put
200 in the second tube.

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:05.710
And the reasons for this are
that the molecules of xenon

00:20:05.710 --> 00:20:07.930
that are in here
are swimming around,

00:20:07.930 --> 00:20:11.470
all they know is that they
have so many joules or watt

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:16.340
seconds per molecule and
that the electrical gradient

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:20.680
in here, which is causing them
to ionize, is exactly the same.

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:23.770
And if you could get out one
xenon molecule from this thing

00:20:23.770 --> 00:20:26.910
and look at it with
an eyeglass, it

00:20:26.910 --> 00:20:29.350
would react exactly the
same in these two tubes.

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:33.570
And it would not know whether
it was in a 6 inch tube or a 12

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:38.190
inch tube or 1,000 inch tube.

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:41.400
So those rules are simple
and straightforward.

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:45.150
And it's all documented
in the technique.

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:46.950
And all I can
guarantee to you when

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:50.350
you use this theory is that
you'll get the wrong answer.

00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:54.010
Because this theory does not
include the electrode effects

00:20:54.010 --> 00:20:56.680
and the wall effects.

00:20:56.680 --> 00:20:58.620
So what you do is
use the theory.

00:20:58.620 --> 00:20:59.530
You can do it quick.

00:20:59.530 --> 00:21:04.070
And you grind out what the
new tube will probably do.

00:21:04.070 --> 00:21:07.220
And do I need to tell you?

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:09.160
You rush into the
laboratory and clip it

00:21:09.160 --> 00:21:11.970
onto one of these various
machines that we have there,

00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:13.720
put the telescope
up, and bang, out

00:21:13.720 --> 00:21:16.720
comes a number, which
will tell you exactly

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:19.030
how far off your theory is.

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:28.360
We have used this technique
a great deal in our various,

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:32.694
development of very
large strobe equipment.

00:21:32.694 --> 00:21:34.360
And it's very useful
there, like the one

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:36.318
up on top of the prudential
building is a tube.

00:21:36.318 --> 00:21:38.240
It's about that long.

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:39.940
And it's a tube about that big.

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:43.570
And there's about 5,000
watt seconds into it.

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:46.610
And just by taking these
dimensional effects,

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:47.480
we can hook it up.

00:21:47.480 --> 00:21:52.500
We can find out what it will
do as an optical device.

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:53.750
We can get the flash duration.

00:21:53.750 --> 00:21:57.590
We can get the efficiency.

00:21:57.590 --> 00:22:00.440
And all these other factors
that we need to know

00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:08.430
can immediately come out
via a preliminary try at it.

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:13.960
Now, the next thing
I have to show you as

00:22:13.960 --> 00:22:15.970
is discussed as a
problem that I do not

00:22:15.970 --> 00:22:18.460
know the answer to very well.

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:21.100
And it still is one of the most
important things in all flash

00:22:21.100 --> 00:22:21.610
equipment.

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:25.110
And that is the
life of the tube.

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:28.270
When most people ask me, what
is the life of the flash lamp?

00:22:28.270 --> 00:22:29.902
I say, well, it's
usually too long.

00:22:29.902 --> 00:22:32.360
Of course, that's the view of
the man in the factory that's

00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:33.900
making them.

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:35.910
He wants it to go on
a lot of production.

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:37.920
But you can make a
flash lamp that will run

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:39.870
for years and years and years.

00:22:39.870 --> 00:22:42.360
In fact, the one on the
Museum of Science, I think,

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:44.580
has been up there about
eight or 10 years.

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:46.020
It quit about five years ago.

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:46.770
And I rushed over.

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:48.090
And I said, glory be.

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:49.810
The thing is finally broke.

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:52.540
But somebody pulled the
plug out of the wall.

00:22:52.540 --> 00:22:53.595
And it is still going.

00:22:53.595 --> 00:22:56.220
And whenever you see the Museum
of Science, that little blinker

00:22:56.220 --> 00:23:01.650
up there, it is, let's see
that curve of efficiency.

00:23:01.650 --> 00:23:05.610
Curve of efficiency, this
is a log plot starting

00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:07.290
at the bottom with one.

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:10.845
You don't have to go
below a one flash.

00:23:10.845 --> 00:23:12.720
I hope none of you will
have that experience,

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:14.710
although it's a lot of fun.

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:16.970
One flash, our lab
is full of pieces.

00:23:16.970 --> 00:23:18.750
Then it goes to 10, 100.

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:20.190
Most of the tubes,
flash tubes are

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:24.810
made have a life of 10 flashes.

00:23:24.810 --> 00:23:28.410
And tubes of this type,
I think that's a 38.

00:23:28.410 --> 00:23:29.814
No, that's a different number.

00:23:29.814 --> 00:23:31.980
That's the tube they're
talking about on that curve.

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:34.950
It's connected in a
series with an inductor.

00:23:34.950 --> 00:23:38.250
And that curve, let's
look at the curve again.

00:23:38.250 --> 00:23:41.670
We run that curve up as
a function of voltage.

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:44.140
And you'll notice, it goes
to 1,000 volts and then 2,000

00:23:44.140 --> 00:23:44.640
volts.

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:47.820
And then at 1, there's
a kind of a star.

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:52.380
That's where we increase
the xenon concentration

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:55.010
in the strobe alley.

00:23:55.010 --> 00:23:59.230
That xenon comes out there
and gives you a increase of,

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:01.790
also tube flies around the room.

00:24:01.790 --> 00:24:04.537
And it's not too
healthy a place.

00:24:04.537 --> 00:24:06.120
If you're going to
do this experiment,

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:08.040
get something between
you and the lamp.

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:10.200
Because the glass
doesn't go very fast.

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:13.760
But it does get in your hair
and causes all kinds of trouble.

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:19.440
Now, the 10, how do you
rate a 10 lamp flash?

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:21.760
It doesn't blow up
after 10 flashes.

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:25.260
What happens is that the
electrodes will melt away.

00:24:25.260 --> 00:24:26.880
The tube will get
dark and black.

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:28.830
And so its efficiency
will reduce.

00:24:28.830 --> 00:24:30.960
So this type of a
lamp, which is used

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:35.090
for stimulating these
very powerful lasers,

00:24:35.090 --> 00:24:37.800
they give us a tremendous whack.

00:24:37.800 --> 00:24:39.560
Its life is only 10 flashes.

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:43.970
Then you throw it out and
put in four or five new ones.

00:24:43.970 --> 00:24:47.720
On the other end of the scale,
at the top end of that curve,

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:50.566
it goes up to 1,000
and 10,000 and 100,000.

00:24:50.566 --> 00:24:51.982
Most of the
electronic flash units

00:24:51.982 --> 00:24:56.880
that are used in small
portable equipments run around,

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:58.070
oh, 100,000.

00:24:58.070 --> 00:24:59.390
They guarantee 10,000.

00:24:59.390 --> 00:25:02.720
But you'll have a hard
time counting after 10,000.

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:04.937
Most of them will
run over 10,000.

00:25:04.937 --> 00:25:06.770
But the ones that you
want to put in beacons

00:25:06.770 --> 00:25:09.320
run one million and 10 million.

00:25:09.320 --> 00:25:12.950
And I think the one over
there on the Museum of Science

00:25:12.950 --> 00:25:17.340
has run actually about
somewhere around 100 million.

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:19.200
And it's still going strong.

00:25:19.200 --> 00:25:24.170
Of course, this is done by
reducing the energy in the tube

00:25:24.170 --> 00:25:25.780
and tending to your business.

00:25:25.780 --> 00:25:30.900
So this curve is a very
interesting one to follow.

00:25:30.900 --> 00:25:34.770
And it's going to become more
important as time goes on.

00:25:34.770 --> 00:25:39.390
Because one of the advantages
of using electronic flash lamp

00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:41.900
as a beacon in a lighthouse
or something is a flash,

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:44.520
is a point that it will
flash for a long, long time

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:46.000
without attention.

00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:48.977
When I was in high school,
I lived in a prairie town

00:25:48.977 --> 00:25:50.560
out in Nebraska that
had a courthouse.

00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:52.170
It went up about
a thousand miles.

00:25:52.170 --> 00:25:54.609
Snow's only 500, 600 feet.

00:25:54.609 --> 00:25:56.400
But one of my jobs as
a high school student

00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:57.900
was to go up there,
open the window,

00:25:57.900 --> 00:26:02.230
and reach out and unscrew a 200
watt bulb when it burned out.

00:26:02.230 --> 00:26:05.580
And I can still remember how,
it looked like it was way up.

00:26:05.580 --> 00:26:10.500
So now that courthouse has
four 10 watt second xenon flash

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:11.310
tubes in it.

00:26:11.310 --> 00:26:15.060
And I service it from Boston
about once every six months.

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:17.070
They keep right on going.

00:26:17.070 --> 00:26:20.100
So I'm still doing the
same job I did in 1920 when

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:21.780
I was a high school student.

00:26:21.780 --> 00:26:24.000
I still climb up that darn
tower and open the window.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:26.560
But I don't have to
reach out so far.

00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:30.480
And if any of you go out
through the prairie in Nebraska,

00:26:30.480 --> 00:26:32.210
there will be one
unique courthouse.

00:26:32.210 --> 00:26:34.560
There's one every 25 miles.

00:26:34.560 --> 00:26:36.030
And that's in rural Nebraska.

00:26:36.030 --> 00:26:39.720
That's the only one out there
with four strobes on it.

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:41.034
So it's quite distinctive.

00:26:41.034 --> 00:26:43.200
In fact, people driving on
the road come trudging in

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:45.075
and say, what are those
blinking lights doing

00:26:45.075 --> 00:26:48.360
on that courthouse?

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:50.340
I'm going to be out
there next week.

00:26:50.340 --> 00:26:52.050
I'm going to take
a few spare tubes.

00:26:52.050 --> 00:26:55.020
And I'll dust them off,
clean them off, put them in.

00:26:55.020 --> 00:26:58.230
But those tubes will
last a long, long time.

00:26:58.230 --> 00:27:01.890
This is very important for
lighthouses and for beacons.

00:27:01.890 --> 00:27:03.680
You don't want to
change them often.

00:27:03.680 --> 00:27:05.610
The tungsten lamp, if
it's used efficiently,

00:27:05.610 --> 00:27:08.420
will never run
more than a month.

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:10.800
If it's used inefficiently,
it'll run longer.

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:13.590
Xenon flash tubes,
of course, will run,

00:27:13.590 --> 00:27:15.900
nobody really has the
patience to find out

00:27:15.900 --> 00:27:19.270
how long they actually will go.

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:22.590
Now I have just a few slides at
the end to kind of wind us up.

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:25.270
And I thought I'd show you a few
pictures, a few applications,

00:27:25.270 --> 00:27:27.260
this one.

00:27:27.260 --> 00:27:31.290
Next slide will show, I'm very
much interested in underwater.

00:27:31.290 --> 00:27:34.270
Here, the strobe is not used
because of its short speed.

00:27:34.270 --> 00:27:38.940
It's not used because if its,
any purpose, but the ability

00:27:38.940 --> 00:27:41.949
to get out a small battery,
enough light to take hundreds

00:27:41.949 --> 00:27:42.990
and hundreds of pictures.

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:45.590
And it's been my pleasure
to work in cameras.

00:27:45.590 --> 00:27:49.550
We've had them down, oh, we've
had them down five miles deep.

00:27:49.550 --> 00:27:52.160
And they're taking pictures
of the bottom of the ocean.

00:27:52.160 --> 00:27:54.319
There's an awful lot of
it to be photographed.

00:27:54.319 --> 00:27:56.360
And we're still working
on this, the angle of it.

00:27:56.360 --> 00:28:00.410
The next slide shows
a color picture

00:28:00.410 --> 00:28:02.990
of a balloon with a
bullet going through it.

00:28:02.990 --> 00:28:04.615
In case any of you
haven't done this,

00:28:04.615 --> 00:28:05.990
when you shoot
through a balloon,

00:28:05.990 --> 00:28:07.760
there's several possibilities.

00:28:07.760 --> 00:28:10.410
If the balloon is only
partially deflated,

00:28:10.410 --> 00:28:12.770
then the bullet
will go in and out.

00:28:12.770 --> 00:28:15.290
And it will not tear.

00:28:15.290 --> 00:28:17.840
The air will just squirt
out through this hole.

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:20.645
And you'll notice that
there's a ring, a wave where,

00:28:20.645 --> 00:28:22.670
it's coming out from
where the bullet went in.

00:28:22.670 --> 00:28:25.610
The other thing is to blow
the balloon up very tight.

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:28.345
And when you shoot the bullet
through it, it will tear.

00:28:28.345 --> 00:28:29.720
If you put the
light around back,

00:28:29.720 --> 00:28:34.630
you'll see a little line going
through where this tear occurs.

00:28:34.630 --> 00:28:40.640
The next slide shows a
golfer with mother flash.

00:28:40.640 --> 00:28:43.250
You've had a chance to do some
of this in the laboratory.

00:28:43.250 --> 00:28:44.930
And I hope you'll
keep this in mind.

00:28:44.930 --> 00:28:46.580
Because this is one of
the most interesting types

00:28:46.580 --> 00:28:47.960
of photography
there is, where you

00:28:47.960 --> 00:28:49.910
put a time sequence of
a whole lot of pictures

00:28:49.910 --> 00:28:51.620
onto a stationary film
so that you've got

00:28:51.620 --> 00:28:52.930
a time record of what goes on.

00:28:52.930 --> 00:28:54.680
And I don't need to
tell you that you have

00:28:54.680 --> 00:28:55.894
to move your lights around.

00:28:55.894 --> 00:28:57.560
So that you do not
light the background,

00:28:57.560 --> 00:28:58.685
you use a black background.

00:28:58.685 --> 00:29:01.580
You use of white subject
and a lot of contrast

00:29:01.580 --> 00:29:03.440
and so forth and so on.

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:05.840
And the last slide,
there's one more

00:29:05.840 --> 00:29:10.000
in there that shows MIT from,
with a 80,000 watt second

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:10.750
strobe.

00:29:10.750 --> 00:29:13.130
There's one of these
hanging in Strobe Alley,

00:29:13.130 --> 00:29:17.100
this one made in 1944
with 80,000 watt seconds

00:29:17.100 --> 00:29:22.700
and two giant tubes in a big
airplane flying up in the sky.

00:29:22.700 --> 00:29:25.760
And someday I hope to build
a real powerful strobe, not

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:28.920
one of these types.

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:30.424
So this concludes this lecture.

00:29:30.424 --> 00:29:32.840
And I hope that all of you
will have some good experiences

00:29:32.840 --> 00:29:35.110
in the laboratory.

